WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.580
Compare a sampled African genome and a sampled Eurasian genome with a Neanderthal

2
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.940
genome. The Eurasian genome shares more derived variants with the Neanderthal.

3
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:22.100
At each site, a chimpanzee shows which allele is ancestral, and a derived variant is

4
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.340
a newer one. Where does the surplus come from?

5
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:28.540
Two different histories could both explain it.

6
00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:36.823
Count the sites. At an ABBA site, the Eurasian and the Neanderthal share the new

7
00:00:36.823 --> 00:00:43.083
variant. At a BABA site, the African and the Neanderthal share it.

8
00:00:44.023 --> 00:00:50.023
Suppose you count 600 ABBA sites and 400 BABA sites.

9
00:00:51.003 --> 00:00:55.823
D is the difference divided by the total, which comes to 0.20.

10
00:00:56.567 --> 00:01:03.027
With no gene flow and one randomly mating ancestor, the model predicts equal counts,

11
00:01:03.327 --> 00:01:08.007
so D equals zero, but here the Eurasian genome shares more.

12
00:01:08.747 --> 00:01:11.927
But do not read D as 20% ancestry.

13
00:01:12.747 --> 00:01:17.287
Old structure in a subdivided African population can also make D positive.

14
00:01:18.127 --> 00:01:20.707
A positive D is consistent with gene flow.

15
00:01:21.227 --> 00:01:22.227
It is not proof.

16
00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.680
Time can separate the two stories.

17
00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:31.580
Recombination cuts DNA that entered from another population into shorter pieces,

18
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.740
every generation. Gene flow tens of thousands of years ago leaves long blocks,

19
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.200
because recombination has had limited time to break them up.

20
00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.280
Old structure leaves only short pieces.

21
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.780
So segment length carries information about timing.

22
00:01:48.683 --> 00:01:55.703
Ust'-Ishim was a Homo sapiens man from western Siberia, who lived about 45,000 years

23
00:01:55.703 --> 00:02:01.123
ago. His genome carried Neanderthal segments longer than those in present-day

24
00:02:01.123 --> 00:02:06.723
people. Dated genomes should carry longer segments if gene flow happened shortly

25
00:02:06.723 --> 00:02:11.583
before they lived, and old structure predicts only a slight change with age.

26
00:02:12.463 --> 00:02:18.383
From the lengths, researchers inferred gene flow 7,000 to 13,000 years before he lived.

27
00:02:19.163 --> 00:02:22.343
That estimate carries the uncertainty of the recombination model.

28
00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:28.447
Fit both histories as explicit models to the same data and compare them.

29
00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:34.047
So the evidence supports Neanderthal gene flow into the ancestors of the sampled non-Africans.

30
00:02:34.047 --> 00:02:39.087
Keep the conclusion to those samples, because it says nothing by itself

31
00:02:39.087 --> 00:02:40.327
about other contacts.

32
00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:42.827
A positive D is a clue.

33
00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:44.607
Test it with segment length.
